---
/**
 * The receipt: what this page actually asked the network for, read from the
 * browser's own record of it, beside what ships in the same artifact and was
 * never asked for.
 *
 * ---- why the page needs one -------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Two of this site's claims are claims about requests. The first is that an
 * adapter a page does not use is never fetched — the whole argument for lazy
 * provider loading, and a sentence a reader has no way to check. The second is
 * the one the page would rather not raise: the hero is a React island, so this
 * page ships React and `react-dom` to a reader in order to argue that the
 * library is small. Both are true and they measure different things, and a page
 * whose thesis is honesty cannot answer the second by staying quiet about it.
 *
 * One instrument answers both, because both are answered by the same evidence:
 * the request log. It is printed in full, largest cost included, and the
 * adapters that never appear in it are printed beside it. A reader who does not
 * believe either claim can open the network panel and compare.
 *
 * ---- what is measured, and what is asserted ---------------------------------
 *
 * Measured, in the reader's browser, from `PerformanceObserver` over `resource`
 * entries: every request, its real transfer size, and whether it arrived after
 * the page had finished loading. Nothing in the upper panel is written by this
 * file. There is no seeded row, no sample and no simulation — the same rule
 * `HeroPlayerIsland.tsx` holds the capability ledger to, and for the same
 * reason: a plausible list would look identical to a reader and to a
 * screenshot, and would be the one untrue thing on the page.
 *
 * Asserted, from the build: the adapter names and their gzipped sizes, which
 * come from `scripts/bundle-budgets.mjs` — the module `pnpm test:budgets` gates
 * with and the module the Budgets section of `/` already prints from. So the
 * list of adapters is derived rather than typed, and an adapter added to the
 * workspace appears here without anyone editing this file.
 *
 * ---- the one thing this component cannot do ---------------------------------
 *
 * It cannot say, of a given request in the log, which package it is. The
 * adapters reach a browser as bundler chunks under hashed names decided AFTER
 * this frontmatter has run — the build cannot hand a component the URL of an
 * artifact the same build has not emitted yet — so mapping `@playdeck/provider-
 * hls` to `/_astro/dist.<hash>.js` is not available from here at any price.
 *
 * What it does instead is refuse to guess. The log prints the real address of
 * every request and never labels one with a package name it inferred, and the
 * lower panel states which adapters the page's own media needs (`used`) and
 * which it cannot need. The live evidence is the log's own count: press the
 * hero and exactly the clip and the one adapter that plays it arrive, tagged
 * `after load`. Four adapters ship and stay unrequested, and the reader can see
 * that nothing else appeared.
 *
 * If the emitted chunk names ever become knowable at build time — a Vite
 * manifest read by a step that runs after the bundle is written, and passed in
 * — the lower panel's `not requested` could become measured rather than
 * derived, and it should be.
 */
import { measureBundles } from '../../../../scripts/bundle-budgets.mjs';

interface Props {
  /**
   * The adapter this page's own media requires, as a package name. It is
   * printed as the one adapter the page can ask for; every other measured
   * adapter is printed as one it cannot. Omit it on a page that mounts no
   * player, and every adapter is listed as unrequested.
   */
  readonly used?: string;
}

const { used } = Astro.props;

// Measured at build time from the module the budget gate measures with, for the
// reason `src/pages/index.astro` gives where it does the same: a page whose
// argument is that a figure is enforced cannot print a second copy of it.
//
// The provider adapters are the report-only half of that module's targets. They
// are selected by their package name rather than by `budget === null`, because
// that field says "not gated" and the question here is "is it an adapter" —
// today the two sets coincide, and a budget added to one adapter should not
// empty this panel.
const adapters = (await measureBundles(import.meta.env.PLAYDECK_REPO_ROOT))
  .filter(({ name }) => name.startsWith('@playdeck/provider-'))
  .map(({ name, size }) => ({
    name,
    size,
    // The page declares which adapter its media needs. Everything else on this
    // list is one the page has no way to ask for.
    needed: name === used
  }));

// A `used` that names nothing measured would print five adapters as unneeded
// while the page quietly fetched one of them, which is exactly the shape of
// wrongness this panel exists to make impossible. Cheaper to fail the build.
if (used !== undefined && !adapters.some(({ needed }) => needed)) {
  throw new Error(
    `Receipt: \`used\` names ${used}, which is not one of the provider adapters ` +
      `scripts/bundle-budgets.mjs measures (${adapters.map(({ name }) => name).join(', ')}).`
  );
}
---

<figure class="receipt" data-receipt>
  <div class="receipt__panel">
    <h2 class="receipt__title">Network receipt</h2>

    <section class="receipt__section">
      <h3 class="receipt__heading">
        Requested by this page
        <span class="receipt__total" data-receipt-total></span>
      </h3>
      {
        /* The list is a real list from first paint and the script replaces its
         * contents once it has something measured to put there. Written this
         * way round rather than as an empty box the script fills, for the
         * reason `DESIGN.md`'s entry-motion section gives about the resting
         * state: what a reader gets when the script does not run has to be the
         * settled, honest thing rather than a hole.
         *
         * So there is no `<noscript>` here. With scripts off the sentence below
         * stays, and it is true — the measurement is the browser's and this
         * page cannot make it without one. The panel underneath needs no script
         * at all and prints the same figures either way. */
      }
      <ol class="receipt__rows" data-receipt-log>
        <li class="receipt__empty">
          Measured in this browser from the Performance API, which needs a
          script to read. Nothing is printed here until one runs. What ships in
          the artifact, below, is measured at build time and is the same either
          way.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="receipt__section">
      <h3 class="receipt__heading">Shipped in this artifact</h3>
      <ul class="receipt__rows">
        {
          adapters.map(({ name, size, needed }) => (
            <li class="receipt__row">
              <span class="receipt__path">{name}</span>
              <span class="receipt__size">{size.toFixed(2)} KB</span>
              <span class="receipt__note">
                {needed
                  ? 'gzipped · fetched on demand, when the clip is loaded'
                  : 'gzipped · nothing on this page can ask for it'}
              </span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="receipt__caption">
    Every address above is one this browser really asked for, in the order it
    asked. The adapter sizes are gzipped and measured at build time by the same
    module that fails the build when a bundle goes over budget.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
  /*
   * The instrument. It reads `resource` entries and prints them; it has no
   * model of the page it is on and no list of anything.
   *
   * `PerformanceObserver` with `buffered: true` rather than a
   * `getEntriesByType` pass plus an observer for the rest. The buffered flag
   * delivers the entries that already exist in the first callback and every
   * later one after it, which is the union the two-call version is assembling —
   * and it has no gap. The two-call version can miss a resource that lands
   * between the read and the `observe`, and a receipt that silently dropped a
   * request would be the one defect this panel cannot have.
   */
  const KB = 1024;
  const kilobytes = (bytes: number) => `${(bytes / KB).toFixed(2)} KB`;

  /**
   * The address, as short as it can be made without becoming ambiguous. Its own
   * origin is dropped because every path would carry the same prefix; anything
   * else keeps its host, because for this page a third-party request is the
   * interesting fact about a row.
   */
  const address = (name: string) => {
    const url = new URL(name, location.href);
    return url.origin === location.origin
      ? url.pathname
      : `${url.host}${url.pathname}`;
  };

  /**
   * What a resource cost, and what the number means.
   *
   * `transferSize` is bytes over the wire including headers, and it is 0 for a
   * resource the browser served from its cache. Printing that 0 as a size would
   * make a reload with a warm cache tell the reader this page weighs nothing,
   * which is the most convincing lie the panel could tell — so a cache hit is
   * printed as a cache hit, with the body size the browser did record beside
   * it.
   *
   * Both sizes zero is a third case and is not a cache hit: a cross-origin
   * response with no `Timing-Allow-Origin` reports nothing, and so does a
   * zero-length body. It says so rather than picking one.
   */
  const cost = (entry: PerformanceResourceTiming) => {
    if (entry.transferSize > 0) {
      return { size: kilobytes(entry.transferSize), note: 'over the wire' };
    }
    if (entry.encodedBodySize > 0) {
      return {
        size: kilobytes(entry.encodedBodySize),
        note: 'from cache · nothing transferred'
      };
    }
    return { size: '—', note: 'size not reported' };
  };

  /*
   * The navigation entry, read once and consulted per row. It is a single live
   * object whose `loadEventEnd` is 0 until the load event has finished, so
   * "after the page had loaded" is a question with a real answer rather than a
   * guess about which observer callback an entry arrived in — a font still in
   * flight when this script runs is not a late request, and a chunk fetched
   * because the reader pressed something is.
   */
  const navigation = performance.getEntriesByType(
    'navigation'
  )[0] as PerformanceNavigationTiming | undefined;

  const afterLoad = (entry: PerformanceResourceTiming) => {
    const loaded = navigation?.loadEventEnd ?? 0;
    return loaded > 0 && entry.startTime > loaded;
  };

  const row = (entry: PerformanceResourceTiming) => {
    const { size, note } = cost(entry);
    const element = document.createElement('li');
    element.className = 'receipt__row';

    const path = document.createElement('span');
    path.className = 'receipt__path';
    path.textContent = address(entry.name);

    const measure = document.createElement('span');
    measure.className = 'receipt__size';
    measure.textContent = size;

    const detail = document.createElement('span');
    detail.className = 'receipt__note';
    if (afterLoad(entry)) {
      element.dataset.late = '';
      const tag = document.createElement('span');
      tag.className = 'receipt__tag';
      tag.textContent = 'after load';
      detail.append(tag, ` · ${entry.initiatorType} · ${note}`);
    } else {
      detail.textContent = `${entry.initiatorType} · ${note}`;
    }

    element.append(path, measure, detail);
    return element;
  };

  for (const receipt of document.querySelectorAll('[data-receipt]')) {
    const log = receipt.querySelector('[data-receipt-log]');
    const total = receipt.querySelector('[data-receipt-total]');
    if (log === null || total === null) continue;

    /*
     * The running totals, kept here rather than recomputed from the DOM. They
     * are sums of what the browser reported, and re-reading them off printed
     * text would be reading this panel's own output back as if it were a
     * measurement.
     */
    let requests = 0;
    let transferred = 0;
    let cached = 0;
    let cachedBytes = 0;
    let printed = false;

    const observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {
      const entries = list.getEntries() as PerformanceResourceTiming[];
      if (entries.length === 0) return;

      // The sentence that stands in for the measurement is replaced by the
      // first measurement, and only then — a script that emptied the list
      // before it had anything to put in it would blank the panel on the one
      // failure it has to survive.
      if (!printed) {
        log.replaceChildren();
        printed = true;
      }

      for (const entry of entries) {
        requests += 1;
        transferred += entry.transferSize;
        if (entry.transferSize === 0 && entry.encodedBodySize > 0) {
          cached += 1;
          cachedBytes += entry.encodedBodySize;
        }
        log.append(row(entry));
      }

      // What came down the wire and what did not, as two figures rather than
      // one. A reload with a warm cache transfers nothing, and a lone total
      // would print `0.00 KB` — a number a reader would take to mean this page
      // is free rather than that they already have it. The second figure is
      // what those resources weigh, so the panel says both.
      const parts = [
        `${requests} ${requests === 1 ? 'request' : 'requests'}`,
        `${kilobytes(transferred)} transferred`
      ];
      if (cached > 0) {
        parts.push(`${kilobytes(cachedBytes)} from cache (${cached})`);
      }
      total.textContent = parts.join(' · ');
    });

    observer.observe({ type: 'resource', buffered: true });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* The same two boxes the hero's panels are built from: a raised body holding
   * a recessed readout. `--elevation-panel` and not `--elevation-instrument` —
   * `DESIGN.md` rule 4 gives the deeper step to the one panel a page is built
   * around, and that panel is the capability ledger. No border, which is the
   * half of the rule that keeps a hairline from reappearing under a shadow. */
  .receipt {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2);
    min-inline-size: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .receipt__panel {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--elevation-panel);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .receipt__panel {
      padding: var(--space-5);
    }
  }

  .receipt__section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    min-inline-size: 0;
  }

  /* The label on an instrument rather than a section title, at the caption rung
   * in the mono — the same treatment `.ledger__title` takes, because the two
   * panels are the same kind of thing and a reader should not have to work out
   * that they are. */
  .receipt__title,
  .receipt__heading {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: var(--weight-regular);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The heading and its running total on one line, with the total pushed to the
   * far end and free to drop under the heading when there is no width for both.
   * `flex-wrap` rather than a media query, because what decides it is the
   * length of a string this panel does not control. */
  .receipt__heading {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  /* The one figure in this panel that is a summary rather than a row, so it
   * takes the full ink the rows' addresses take rather than the subtle ink of
   * the words beside it. */
  .receipt__total {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .receipt__rows {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    padding: var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
    background-color: var(--color-sunken);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* ---- why the five row selectors below are `:global()` ---------------------
   *
   * Astro scopes a component's rules by stamping an attribute onto the elements
   * in its own template, and half the elements these rules address are not in
   * it: every row in the upper panel is built by the script above with
   * `createElement`, which no scope attribute reaches. So the choice is not
   * between scoped and global rules, it is between global rules and rules that
   * match nothing — and this was caught by the measured panel rendering as a
   * paragraph of run-together text at 320px while the build panel beside it,
   * whose rows are in the template, laid out correctly.
   *
   * `:global()` per selector rather than `<style is:global>`, which is the
   * spelling `HeroPlayer.astro` already uses for the same reason. Each of these
   * class names appears nowhere else on the site, which is what makes it safe,
   * and the same five rules dress both panels' rows — the built ones and the
   * measured ones — so the two cannot drift into looking like different kinds
   * of thing.
   *
   * An entry, not a table row, for the reason `.row` in `HeroPlayer.astro` is
   * one: the address is the part with no fixed length, and giving the note a
   * column of its own would strand it against the far edge. The address and its
   * size share the first line; what qualifies the size sits under both.
   *
   * Nothing here animates. A row appears the moment the browser records the
   * request, which is the behaviour this panel exists to show, and `DESIGN.md`
   * puts this app's animation count at two — both on `/`, neither of them this.
   * So there is no motion for `prefers-reduced-motion` to suppress, and a
   * suppressed transition cannot leave a request unprinted. */
  :global(.receipt__row) {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    column-gap: var(--space-3);
    row-gap: var(--space-1);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  /* A hashed chunk name has no spaces in it and is longer than a 320px column,
   * so it breaks mid-token rather than pushing the panel wider than the page.
   * `anywhere` and not `break-word`: the latter leaves the line box sized to the
   * unbroken word for min-content purposes, which is the width this is trying
   * not to have. */
  :global(.receipt__path) {
    min-inline-size: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  :global(.receipt__size) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The bottom rung, in rem so a reader who raised their root size gets a
   * larger floor rather than the same one. `DESIGN.md` has nothing beneath it.
   */
  :global(.receipt__note) {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-fn);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-fn);
  }

  /* The tag on a request the reader caused. It is picked out by ink weight
   * rather than by colour: the three capability colours carry a domain meaning
   * — available, unknown, unavailable — and "arrived late" is not a point on
   * that axis. Spending one of them here would say that it is. */
  :global(.receipt__tag) {
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }

  /* What stands in the readout before a measurement exists, and what stays
   * there when no script runs. Prose rather than machine output, because it is
   * this page speaking rather than the browser — so it is the sans face, and it
   * is the only thing inside a readout on this panel that is. */
  .receipt__empty {
    max-inline-size: var(--measure);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }

  .receipt__caption {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--color-ink-subtle);
  }
</style>
